    "disease_Bacterial_spot_name": "Bacterial Spot",
    "disease_Bacterial_spot_description": "Bacterial spot is caused by Xanthomonas bacteria. It affects leaves, stems, and fruit, causing dark spots and lesions that reduce yield and fruit quality.",
    "disease_Bacterial_spot_symptoms": "Small dark brown to black spots on leaves\nYellow halos around spots\nLeaf drop in severe cases\nRaised, scab-like lesions on fruit",
    "disease_Bacterial_spot_recommendations": "Remove and destroy infected plant parts\nApply copper-based bactericides\nAvoid overhead watering\nEnsure good air circulation\nUse disease-free seeds and transplants",
    "disease_Early_blight_name": "Early Blight",
    "disease_Early_blight_description": "Early blight is caused by the fungus Alternaria solani. It typically affects older, lower leaves first and can cause significant defoliation and reduced fruit quality.",
    "disease_Early_blight_symptoms": "Dark brown spots with concentric rings (target pattern)\nYellowing tissue around spots\nStarts on lower leaves and moves upward\nPremature leaf drop",
    "disease_Early_blight_recommendations": "Remove affected leaves immediately\nApply fungicide spray regularly\nImprove air circulation by spacing plants\nMulch around plants to prevent soil splash\nRotate crops every 2-3 years",
    "disease_Late_blight_name": "Late Blight",
    "disease_Late_blight_description": "Late blight is caused by the oomycete Phytophthora infestans. It spreads rapidly in cool, wet weather and can devastate an entire crop within days.",
    "disease_Late_blight_symptoms": "Large, irregular water-soaked patches on leaves\nWhite fuzzy growth on the underside of leaves\nDark brown blotches on stems\nRapid plant collapse in humid conditions",
    "disease_Late_blight_recommendations": "Remove and destroy all infected plants immediately\nApply preventive fungicides before wet weather\nDo not compost infected material\nAvoid overhead irrigation\nPlant resistant varieties when possible",
    "disease_Leaf_Mold_name": "Leaf Mold",
    "disease_Leaf_Mold_description": "Leaf mold is caused by the fungus Passalora fulva (Cladosporium fulvum). It thrives in warm, humid greenhouse conditions and can reduce photosynthesis.",
    "disease_Leaf_Mold_symptoms": "Pale green to yellow spots on upper leaf surfaces\nOlive-green to grayish-purple fuzzy growth on undersides\nLeaves curl, wither, and drop prematurely\nMost common in high-humidity environments",
    "disease_Leaf_Mold_recommendations": "Increase ventilation in greenhouses\nReduce humidity below 85%\nRemove infected leaves promptly\nApply fungicides if the outbreak is severe\nUse resistant tomato varieties",
    "disease_Septoria_leaf_spot_name": "Septoria Leaf Spot",
    "disease_Septoria_leaf_spot_description": "Septoria leaf spot is caused by the fungus Septoria lycopersici. It typically appears after fruit set and can cause extensive defoliation.",
    "disease_Septoria_leaf_spot_symptoms": "Small circular spots with dark brown borders\nGray or tan centers with tiny black dots (pycnidia)\nStarts on lower leaves and moves upward\nSevere defoliation if untreated",
    "disease_Septoria_leaf_spot_recommendations": "Remove and destroy infected leaves\nApply fungicide treatments regularly\nMulch around plants to prevent soil splash\nAvoid overhead watering\nStake or cage plants for better airflow",
    "disease_Target_Spot_name": "Target Spot",
    "disease_Target_Spot_description": "Target spot is caused by the fungus Corynespora cassiicola. It affects leaves, stems, and fruit, causing circular lesions with concentric rings.",
    "disease_Target_Spot_symptoms": "Small brown spots that enlarge into target-like rings\nLesions may merge, causing large necrotic areas\nPremature defoliation of lower canopy\nFruit lesions with sunken, dark spots",
    "disease_Target_Spot_recommendations": "Remove and destroy infected plant debris\nApply appropriate fungicides preventively\nImprove air circulation between plants\nPractice crop rotation with non-solanaceous crops\nAvoid excessive nitrogen fertilisation",
    "disease_Spider_mites_name": "Spider Mites (Two-Spotted)",
    "disease_Spider_mites_description": "Two-spotted spider mites (Tetranychus urticae) are tiny arachnids that feed on plant sap, causing stippling, yellowing, and reduced vigour. They thrive in hot, dry conditions.",
    "disease_Spider_mites_symptoms": "Tiny yellow or white stippling on leaves\nFine silken webbing on leaf undersides\nLeaves turn bronze or yellow and become brittle\nSevere infestations cause leaf drop and plant stress",
    "disease_Spider_mites_recommendations": "Spray plants with a strong jet of water\nApply insecticidal soap or neem oil\nIncrease ambient humidity around plants\nRemove heavily infested leaves and destroy them\nIntroduce beneficial predatory mites",
    "disease_Yellow_Leaf_Curl_Virus_name": "Yellow Leaf Curl Virus",
    "disease_Yellow_Leaf_Curl_Virus_description": "Tomato Yellow Leaf Curl Virus (TYLCV) is transmitted by whiteflies (Bemisia tabaci). It causes severe stunting, leaf curling, and dramatic yield loss.",
    "disease_Yellow_Leaf_Curl_Virus_symptoms": "Upward curling of leaf margins\nYellowing (chlorosis) between leaf veins\nSeverely stunted plant growth\nSmall, distorted, cupped leaves",
    "disease_Yellow_Leaf_Curl_Virus_recommendations": "Control whitefly populations with yellow sticky traps\nApply appropriate insecticides against whiteflies\nRemove and destroy infected plants early\nUse TYLCV-resistant tomato varieties\nUse reflective mulches to deter whiteflies",
    "disease_Tomato_mosaic_virus_name": "Tomato Mosaic Virus",
    "disease_Tomato_mosaic_virus_description": "Tomato Mosaic Virus (ToMV) is a highly stable virus spread through contaminated tools, hands, and seeds. It causes mottled leaves and can persist in soil debris for years.",
    "disease_Tomato_mosaic_virus_symptoms": "Light and dark green mosaic patterns on leaves\nLeaf curling and distortion\nStunted plant growth\nReduced and uneven fruit ripening",
    "disease_Tomato_mosaic_virus_recommendations": "Remove and destroy infected plants immediately\nDisinfect tools and hands with milk or bleach solution\nUse certified virus-free seeds and transplants\nAvoid tobacco use near plants (cross-contamination)\nPlant ToMV-resistant varieties",
    "disease_healthy_name": "Healthy Tomato",
    "disease_healthy_description": "Your tomato plant appears healthy with no visible signs of disease or pest damage. Continue with regular care and monitoring to maintain plant health.",
    "disease_healthy_symptoms": "Vibrant green leaves with no spots\nStrong, upright stem growth\nNo discoloration or wilting\nNormal fruit development",
    "disease_healthy_recommendations": "Continue regular watering schedule\nMaintain balanced fertilisation\nMonitor plants weekly for early signs of trouble\nEnsure good air circulation between plants\nPractice crop rotation each season",
    "disease_Unknown_name": "Unknown Condition",
    "disease_Unknown_description": "The model could not confidently identify a specific disease. Please ensure the image is clear, well-lit, and contains a tomato plant leaf.",
    "disease_Unknown_symptoms": "Unclear or blurry image\nImage may not contain a tomato leaf\nLow-confidence prediction from the model",
    "disease_Unknown_recommendations": "Take a clearer, well-lit photo\nEnsure the leaf fills most of the frame\nAvoid shadows and glare on the leaf surface\nConsult an agricultural expert if symptoms persist"
